WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.847
Welcome to Module 5 of the
Data Platform and SQL Server

2
00:00:03.847 --> 00:00:07.438
Identity learning path: groups,
roles and the perimeter.

3
00:00:07.438 --> 00:00:11.263
You sort every Active Directory
group that reaches Sequel Server into

4
00:00:11.263 --> 00:00:15.138
its Entra replacement, and keep
privileged roles off rule-based groups.

5
00:00:15.138 --> 00:00:17.264
The module follows runbook R seven,

6
00:00:17.264 --> 00:00:21.737
and Chapter 5 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

7
00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:23.820
Take it after Module 4.

8
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:28.957
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:28.957 --> 00:00:32.070
Group-based access survives
the move to Entra I D.

10
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.245
What changes is who keeps each group
current, and what stands in front of it.

11
00:00:36.245 --> 00:00:39.482
A group kept by hand from
the org chart becomes a rule.

12
00:00:39.482 --> 00:00:43.220
A group of named administrators
becomes an assigned group under pim.

13
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.070
And a distribution list
never becomes a login.

14
00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:50.634
In this module you take every Active
Directory group that reaches Sequel Server,

15
00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:53.045
and say which Entra group replaces it.

16
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.577
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

17
00:00:59.577 --> 00:01:02.127
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

18
00:01:02.127 --> 00:01:06.669
You'll name the four Active Directory
group patterns in the data platform book,

19
00:01:06.669 --> 00:01:10.978
Data Platform: SQL Server Identity,
and the Entra replacement for each.

20
00:01:10.978 --> 00:01:14.706
You'll explain why a dynamic rule
reads an H R maintained code,

21
00:01:14.706 --> 00:01:17.390
and excludes disabled accounts and guests.

22
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.801
You'll run the kit's
group map over fixtures,

23
00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:24.002
and read its flags for nesting and for
a privileged role on a dynamic group.

24
00:01:24.002 --> 00:01:28.477
And you'll say which check enforces one
group, one role, and why the map doesn't.

25
00:01:28.477 --> 00:01:30.398
Before you start, finish Module 4:

26
00:01:30.398 --> 00:01:34.166
its login plan is where the
one-group-one-role warning comes from.

27
00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:36.180
Lab 1 needs no tenant.

28
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.893
Unit 2 starts with how a
group reaches the engine.

29
00:01:42.893 --> 00:01:46.165
An Entra group reaches Sequel
Server as a group login,

30
00:01:46.165 --> 00:01:48.355
then a database user added to a role.

31
00:01:48.355 --> 00:01:51.115
The data platform book's
Chapter 16, Groups,

32
00:01:51.115 --> 00:01:55.530
Roles and How Membership Reaches
the Engine, makes one rule central.

33
00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:59.048
Each Entra group grants exactly
one role, on one database,

34
00:01:59.048 --> 00:02:02.567
in one environment, so the
group's name says what it grants.

35
00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:06.520
The model stays flat, because
dynamic groups can't contain groups,

36
00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.130
and nesting builds on behavior that is
unsupported, ending or undocumented.

37
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.630
Members are listed directly,
by rule or by assignment.

38
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.097
One group, one role is checked
where the roles are known:

39
00:02:18.097 --> 00:02:20.255
the login plan from Module 4 warns.

40
00:02:20.255 --> 00:02:22.734
The group map carries grants per instance,

41
00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:26.090
not roles per database, so
it doesn't repeat the check.

42
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.940
Each legacy group then lands
in one of four patterns.

43
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:38.502
Pattern one: organization-derived groups,
kept from the org chart, become dynamic.

44
00:02:38.502 --> 00:02:43.531
The kit writes the rule on the H R
organization code in extension attribute ten,

45
00:02:43.531 --> 00:02:47.941
with clauses that require an enabled
account and a member, not a guest.

46
00:02:47.941 --> 00:02:50.177
Never on department or job title.

47
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:54.649
Pattern two: explicit role groups
become assigned, managed by a delegated

48
00:02:54.649 --> 00:02:57.435
administrator, and
eligible through pim when

49
00:02:57.435 --> 00:03:00.074
the group holds sys-admin
or security admin.

50
00:03:00.074 --> 00:03:03.227
Pattern three: distribution
lists become mail-only.

51
00:03:03.227 --> 00:03:07.653
Update the Sequel Server Agent operator's
address, and give it no login.

52
00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:11.920
Pattern four: mail-enabled security
groups split in two, a mail-only group

53
00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.615
and an assigned security group, with the
pairing recorded in both descriptions.

54
00:03:16.615 --> 00:03:18.878
And when a group's codes aren't recorded,

55
00:03:18.878 --> 00:03:22.720
the target says to ask H R for
the code before writing the rule.

56
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.120
Unit 3 is the attribute,
pim and the perimeter.

57
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.660
Under dynamic membership,

58
00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:35.345
whoever can write the attribute
a rule reads controls access.

59
00:03:35.345 --> 00:03:40.303
So the book's Chapter 17 has rules read
H R owned codes, such as the organization

60
00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:45.107
code in extension attribute ten, never
display strings such as department.

61
00:03:45.107 --> 00:03:48.633
And every access rule excludes
disabled accounts and guests.

62
00:03:48.633 --> 00:03:51.357
Groups that hold sys-admin
are never dynamic.

63
00:03:51.357 --> 00:03:55.219
Microsoft Learn says pim for Groups
doesn't work on dynamic groups,

64
00:03:55.219 --> 00:03:57.746
or on groups synchronized
from on premises.

65
00:03:57.746 --> 00:04:00.745
So privileged groups are
cloud-only and assigned.

66
00:04:00.745 --> 00:04:04.793
Otherwise, whoever can write the
attribute could make someone sys-admin.

67
00:04:04.793 --> 00:04:08.032
Conditional Access then sits
in front of every user token.

68
00:04:08.032 --> 00:04:12.351
But it applies to users, not to
managed identities, so applications are

69
00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:17.120
covered by the application chain in
Chapter 7, not by Conditional Access.

70
00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:21.807
Try it.

71
00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:26.758
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7, from the kit's
folder, on the kit's fictional estate.

72
00:04:26.758 --> 00:04:29.091
First, make this run's results folder,

73
00:04:29.091 --> 00:04:33.058
named for the time you start, so
every run keeps its own results.

74
00:04:33.058 --> 00:04:35.270
Step one maps the estate's groups.

75
00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.620
There's one group of each pattern, and a
second dynamic group with a nested group.

76
00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.882
Contoso payroll users and Contoso
H R analysts become dynamic groups,

77
00:04:43.882 --> 00:04:46.233
each rule on its own organization code,

78
00:04:46.233 --> 00:04:49.907
with the clauses that exclude
disabled accounts and guests.

79
00:04:49.907 --> 00:04:54.120
Contoso sequel admins becomes an
assigned group, eligible through pim.

80
00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.670
The agent alerts list becomes
mail-only, with no login.

81
00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.745
And the claims team splits in two.

82
00:04:59.745 --> 00:05:05.545
Notice the flag on H R analysts: nested,
flatten, because it contains H R leads.

83
00:05:05.545 --> 00:05:08.625
The rules are long, so
PowerShell drops the flags

84
00:05:08.625 --> 00:05:12.100
column from the table on
screen; step two prints it.

85
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.212
Step two reads what each group grants.

86
00:05:18.212 --> 00:05:22.487
Contoso sequel admins holds
sys-admin on sequel P R D oh one.

87
00:05:22.487 --> 00:05:24.738
The distribution list grants nothing.

88
00:05:24.738 --> 00:05:27.588
And the nested flag shows on H R analysts.

89
00:05:27.588 --> 00:05:29.937
Now look at Contoso payroll users.

90
00:05:29.937 --> 00:05:33.212
It reaches two databases
on sequel P R D oh one.

91
00:05:33.212 --> 00:05:37.763
The data platform book's rule is
one group, one role, one database.

92
00:05:37.763 --> 00:05:39.524
The map doesn't check roles.

93
00:05:39.524 --> 00:05:41.173
Module 4's login plan does:

94
00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:45.175
its warning for the payroll users
group names the three roles.

95
00:05:45.175 --> 00:05:48.330
Split a group like that before
the mapping is approved.

96
00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:56.005
Step three pretends the sys-admin
group were kept by the org chart.

97
00:05:56.005 --> 00:06:00.182
It loads the kit's module, reads
Contoso sequel admins from the fixture,

98
00:06:00.182 --> 00:06:02.442
and marks its membership as org-chart.

99
00:06:02.442 --> 00:06:05.855
The map now sorts it as
pattern one, a dynamic group.

100
00:06:05.855 --> 00:06:08.105
The group has no organization codes,

101
00:06:08.105 --> 00:06:12.005
so the target says to ask H R for
the code rather than write a rule.

102
00:06:12.005 --> 00:06:15.893
But the flag is what matters: a
privileged role on a dynamic group.

103
00:06:15.893 --> 00:06:18.255
Use an assigned group under pim instead.

104
00:06:18.255 --> 00:06:20.960
A privileged role never sits on a rule.

105
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.935
Step four opens the manifest.

106
00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:30.144
The step is groups, its mode is read-only,

107
00:06:30.144 --> 00:06:33.810
and it supports A C two and
A C six, with one K S I.

108
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.272
It records the hash of each file it wrote.

109
00:06:36.272 --> 00:06:40.123
Step five runs this chapter's
tests: four pass, none fail.

110
00:06:40.123 --> 00:06:42.023
And step six lists your runs.

111
00:06:42.023 --> 00:06:43.561
There's nothing to clean up.

112
00:06:43.561 --> 00:06:45.622
The kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

113
00:06:45.622 --> 00:06:48.097
and this run's results
stay in its own folder.

114
00:06:48.097 --> 00:06:52.506
Check your work: you can say which Entra
group replaces each of the estate's

115
00:06:52.506 --> 00:06:57.000
five groups, and why the map refuses
to make the sys-admin group dynamic.

116
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.350
Lab 2 compares the map with a real tenant.

117
00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.088
It's not run by the authors.

118
00:07:05.088 --> 00:07:07.745
Use a test tenant whose
groups are synchronized

119
00:07:07.745 --> 00:07:09.738
from a test domain, never production.

120
00:07:09.738 --> 00:07:14.188
You need PowerShell 7 with the Microsoft
Graph PowerShell S D K's groups module,

121
00:07:14.188 --> 00:07:18.229
an account that can consent to the
delegated group read all permission,

122
00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.612
and a groups file for your test domain's
groups, in the shape of the kit's fixture.

123
00:07:22.612 --> 00:07:24.463
Map your test groups with the kit.

124
00:07:24.463 --> 00:07:27.074
Sign in to Microsoft Graph, read-only.

125
00:07:27.074 --> 00:07:32.225
A Microsoft 365 G C C tenant uses
the worldwide Graph endpoint.

126
00:07:32.225 --> 00:07:36.616
List the tenant's groups with the properties
that decide the pattern, put the kit's

127
00:07:36.616 --> 00:07:40.875
answer beside what the tenant has, and
look for cloud groups that already exist.

128
00:07:40.875 --> 00:07:43.063
Nothing in the lab writes to the tenant.

129
00:07:43.063 --> 00:07:47.390
To back it out, sign out of Graph
and delete the lab's output folder.

130
00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.790
The federal requirement.

131
00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:59.422
Chapter 5's table maps the group
map to NIST SP 800-53 A C two,

132
00:07:59.422 --> 00:08:03.515
Account Management, and A C six,
Least Privilege, with one K S I.

133
00:08:03.515 --> 00:08:07.958
The map provides evidence for both: which
group grants what, how its membership

134
00:08:07.958 --> 00:08:11.990
is kept, and which privileged groups
are eligible rather than standing.

135
00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:16.586
The I S S O keeps the groups file and
its manifest, which records the step,

136
00:08:16.586 --> 00:08:19.598
its controls and K S Is,
and the hash of each file;

137
00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:23.877
the Lab 2 comparison; and the record
of which groups went under pim.

138
00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:28.165
Runbook R seven lists the evidence each
state of the provisioning phase needs.

139
00:08:28.165 --> 00:08:32.393
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

140
00:08:32.393 --> 00:08:35.853
your providers report against
them, the agency doesn't.

141
00:08:35.853 --> 00:08:39.584
The agency still sets the
parameters, policy and procedures,

142
00:08:39.584 --> 00:08:43.470
and the assessor decides whether
each control is in place.

143
00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.320
Check yourself with two questions.

144
00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:53.483
First: Contoso H R analysts
contains Contoso H R leads.

145
00:08:53.483 --> 00:08:56.044
Why does the map flag it nested, flatten?

146
00:08:56.044 --> 00:08:57.445
The answer is a.

147
00:08:57.445 --> 00:09:01.845
A dynamic rule can't select groups, so
the inner group's members would drop out.

148
00:09:01.845 --> 00:09:07.220
Second: Contoso payroll users reaches
two databases on sequel P R D oh one.

149
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.633
Which check catches the
one-group-one-role problem?

150
00:09:09.633 --> 00:09:14.632
The answer is b: Chapter 4's login
plan, whose warning names the roles.

151
00:09:14.632 --> 00:09:18.295
The map carries grants per
instance, not roles per database.

152
00:09:18.295 --> 00:09:22.940
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

153
00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:27.765
To sum up.

154
00:09:27.765 --> 00:09:32.069
You sorted the fictional estate's groups
into dynamic, assigned, mail-only

155
00:09:32.069 --> 00:09:36.352
and split targets, and saw the map refuse
to put a privileged role on a rule.

156
00:09:36.352 --> 00:09:41.267
Group logins, pim and Conditional Access
now decide who reaches Sequel Server,

157
00:09:41.267 --> 00:09:44.140
in place of hand-kept
Active Directory groups.

158
00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:47.190
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 5's checklist.

159
00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:51.480
Next, Module 6: N T L M and the S P Ns.
